title The post standing on the line -- three families cut a whole into pieces, the tightest carried it by exactly zero, and the compiler holds the relation now
prompt The standing Silence seat: open on ListAgents and the cold roster, reds first, council row N mod 5, QA on touch, twice-pulled send, path-limited signing, stop at custody gates.
think OTHER HAND BEFORE A FILE MOVED. ListAgents named one peer, grain-55, interactive, 58 minutes old. HEAD clean on main, no stash, empty index, `git stash list` empty -- the readings %321 and %364 ask for, taken before anything was read.
think REDS FIRST, AND BOTH OPEN ROWS ARE GATES. `%374` is my own from the previous lap and waits on Keaton's word; `%360`'s remainder is the seed-subject spelling at custody gate %1 plus red guards in lanes this seat does not hold. Nothing agent-closable, so the door stayed the card's: Comlink's other bounds.
think ROTA ROW 1, AND IT CHOSE THE LAP. N = 3846 by `git rev-list --count HEAD`, and 3846 mod 5 is 1 -- AIR, Saturn, law and boundary, the row that feels. The previous lap read row 0 by hand, so row 1 follows without a repeat. Its threshold page says the thing this lap turned out to be: *a claim of a boundary is tested by pressing on it; if the hand passes through, the boundary was a wish, and the lap writes that down.* Touch only works at a surface. You meet a thing where it ends.
think THE CRUX, FOUND BY PRESSING RATHER THAN BY READING. Comlink's first desk landed `max_wire_payload=340` last lap. The obvious next bound was another literal; the one that mattered was a RELATION. Three families in this tree cut a whole into pieces and carry them, and each publishes a trio -- the whole's ceiling, one piece's size, the most pieces allowed. For a whole the module ACCEPTS to cross, `whole <= pieces * piece` must hold. It held in all three, stated in no room, no comment, and no guard.
think MEASURED WITH THE COMPILER RATHER THAN BY HAND. I built the three families in a throwaway pen and printed their constants rather than adding up literals: batch 4096 <= 16 * 331 = 5296, slack 1200; vessel 1024 <= 8 * 267 = 2136, slack 1112; bead 512 <= 8 * 64 = 512, slack ZERO.
think THE ZERO IS THE FINDING. `mantra/beading.rye` accepts a 512-byte resin and its content-defined chunker cuts no piece shorter than `cdc_min_bead` = 64, with `max_beads` = 8. Eight sixty-fours is five hundred and twelve exactly. Both numbers holding that post on the line are the kind a routine lap moves: `cdc_min_bead` sits among the CHUNKER'S TUNING constants beside a hash mask and a comment about average piece size, filed under how the cutting behaves rather than under how much fits.
think SO I PRESSED, AND THE HAND WENT THROUGH. Lowering `cdc_min_bead` 64 -> 32 in a pen and changing nothing else: the same legal 512-byte resin comes back `error.TooManyBeads` -- refused by the module that accepted it two lines earlier.
think AND THEN I MEASURED THE HARM RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT. Raising `max_resin_bytes` 512 -> 576 instead, I fed 3,999 pseudorandom wholes through `bead_content_defined`: 9 refused, roughly one in 444. The control at the seated bound refused 0 of 3,999 with the worst content reaching exactly 8 beads. That pair is the whole argument -- the failure is CONTENT-DEPENDENT, so 3,990 of those wholes pass and a single-fixture test ships it.
obs THE RELATION IS TIED AT COMPTIME IN THE TWO ROOMS THIS LANE HOLDS, and every tie was proven able to refuse before it shipped. `mantra/beading.rye` carries three -- the content-defined path, the fixed-size path, and the claim its own `max_beads` comment has made since `20260706` that a full bead-index is itself a legal resin. `mantra/recall_batch_wire.rye` carries one, reaching across into `resin_batch`'s ceiling. Seven askings on metal: four inverted ties stop the build, and so do the three PLAUSIBLE REAL EDITS -- `cdc_min_bead` to 32, `max_resin_bytes` to 576, `max_batch_chunks` halved. The two that were measured to refuse a legal resin now refuse to compile.
obs A RATCHET RATHER THAN A RED, BY THE LAW'S OWN WORDING, and I say why rather than leave it read as a judgment call. Nothing here produced a wrong answer: all three relations hold today. A red is something wrong; a ratchet is something not yet uniform and books nothing. This was the second -- the same reading `%369`'s own lap took one bound over -- so it is displayed on a desk, tied at comptime, and named on the card rather than given a ledger row.
obs ONE THING WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, AND IT IS A COMMENT. `beading.rye` read *"Eight 32-byte digests plus the 4-byte header make a 292-byte index."* The header is `index_header_len` = 36, not 4, and 8*32+4 is 260 -- a reader recomputing from that sentence would conclude there is room for more beads than there is. The conclusion, 292, is right; the stated part is wrong. Repaired on touch under docs-implementation-sync, and the new comptime block is now what checks the claim rather than the sentence. Not booked: the program's behaviour was never wrong and this is one lantern rather than a loom, so it is named here for Keaton to overrule.
obs THE SCAN HOLDS NO VALUE OF ITS OWN, AND THE SLACK IS REPORTED RATHER THAN GATED. It reads the trios the desk displays, reads the same numbers out of the Rye, composes `max_chunk_body` from its named parts the way the compiler does, and computes the relation. Control case 12 gives beading real headroom -- `cdc_min_bead` to 128, slack 0 -> 512 -- and WALKS FREE, because a guard that reds on an improvement is a guard someone turns off. Case 13 raises a batch exactly to its capacity and also walks free.
obs THE THREE CASES THAT MATTER MOST KEEP THE DESK HONEST WHILE BREAKING THE CODE. Cases 9-11 plant the real edits AND update the desk to match, so `rows_agree` stays yes and only the arithmetic sees it. That is the shape of the failure this guard exists for: two rooms in perfect agreement about a number that cannot work.
obs A HOLE I FOUND IN MY OWN SCAN AND CLOSED. The desk DISPLAYS a capacity and a slack column beside each trio, and the first draft read the trio and computed capacity itself, leaving the desk's own displayed arithmetic unchecked. A teaching surface that lies in its own table teaches the wrong number, so `desk_capacity_agrees` reads it, proven from both sides by cases 14 and 15.
obs BSD SED HAS NO ALTERNATION, AND MY FIRST DRAFT FAILED OPEN BECAUSE OF IT. `sed -n 's/^::    \(batch\|vessel\|bead\) .../p'` matched nothing on this bench and read `desk_families=0` -- a guard silently reading an empty table. It is the same class the card names for Mystery's module-label guard, met from the inside. The row match is `grep -E` now, and the reason is written above the line so the next hand does not restore the sed.
obs MY OWN TALLIES WERE WRONG AND THE CONTROL CAUGHT THEM, which is the second time this family has taught that lesson. I asserted 15 refusals and 5 welcomes for twenty cases; the truth is 16 and 4. The control refused itself until the arithmetic matched, exactly as `20260830.165042` recorded it doing.
obs THE COLD PASS RAN LONG AND I SAY WHAT IT ACTUALLY READ. It opened clean -- `staged_uncommitted=0`, `stashed_entries=0`, `roster_receipt=none` -- and reached the readings recorded in the loom line below before this round needed to write. The one red it found is `rule_twin`, which is custody gate %7 and named on the card. No receipt was written, and by `%374` none can be while a gated red counts as a red.
obs THE SYMLINK LANE BOUNDARY HELD WHERE THE PREVIOUS LAP DREW IT. `comlink/` reaches into `mantra/` and `amphora/` through symlinks, so a lap working "Comlink" can edit three rooms without noticing. Both tied files are Mantra's, which is this seat's lane. `amphora/vessel_fetch_wire.rye` carries the third family in the same shape with 1,112 bytes of slack: read, measured, displayed on the desk, and left untouched. Control case 19 proves that room can tie its own half and walk free, because the tie count is a FLOOR rather than an equality.
obs THE SEND FOUND A SECOND RED, AND IT IS TWO CORRECT MECHANISMS COMPOSING WRONG. My guarded Git-nib amend refused with `index not empty` over a tree `git status` showed as clean but for two `MM` pages. `tools/hooks/pre-commit` regenerates `README.md`'s metrics and `docs-geode/libraries/README.md` and stages each with `git add`, while this seat commits path-limited, so Git builds the commit from its own temporary index. HEAD and the worktree both carried the fresh 1750, proven identical by SHA-256; the real index kept the pre-hook 1749. The amend guard of `%255`/`%331` reads that index and refused a commit nothing was wrong with.
obs PROVEN BOTH WAYS IN A PEN RATHER THAN REASONED ABOUT, because a red booked on a theory is worse than a finding named. One hook that rewrites and stages a file, committed WITH a pathspec: HEAD `gen 2`, index `gen 1`, worktree `gen 2`, `--cached` dirty. The identical hook committed WITHOUT a pathspec: all three `gen 2`, index clean. The pathspec is the whole difference, and that is `%376`.
obs NAMED, NOT TAKEN, AND THE REASON IS THE SURFACE. Three repairs are open -- the hook refreshing the index after staging, the amend guard reading HEAD's tree rather than the index, or the seat committing whole-index after staging precisely. The first two are read by every body in the fleet and the third is this seat's own prompt, so none is a lap's to take at its close. This round worked around it by `git restore --staged` on exactly the two paths, after proving HEAD and worktree byte-identical, then amended without a pathspec so it did not re-create the defect it had just booked.
